<h2 id="gig-apps">Driving, delivery and errand apps</h2>
<p>These are the highest-earning phone gigs per hour of effort, because you are paid for physical work. Your phone is the office: you accept orders, navigate, message customers and cash out from the app. If you are deciding between platforms, our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft vs Uber guide</a> covers sign-up requirements and how pay is calculated.</p>

<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="food-delivery">1. Food delivery</h3>
    <p>DoorDash, Uber Eats and Grubhub let you go online whenever you want. Lunch and dinner rushes, rainy evenings and weekends pay best. Running two apps at once and declining low-paying orders is how experienced drivers keep their hourly rate up.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$25/hr gross</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Car, bike or scooter</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible evenings</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="grocery-shopping">2. Grocery shopping</h3>
    <p>Instacart and Shipt pay you to shop and deliver. Big orders with large tips are the jackpot; small orders across town are not worth taking. Knowing your local store layouts makes you noticeably faster.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$25/hr gross</li><li><b>Startup:</b> A car</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Daytime hours</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">3. Rideshare driving</h3>
    <p>Uber and Lyft generally pay more per hour than food delivery, especially around airports, concerts and late weekend nights. You need a newer four-door car and a clean driving record, and insurance and wear costs are higher.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $18–$30/hr gross</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Qualifying car</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Weekend nights</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="amazon-flex">4. Amazon Flex</h3>
    <p>You reserve delivery blocks of a few hours in the app and deliver packages along a set route. The pay is predictable per block, but popular blocks get claimed within seconds in many markets.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Often $18–$25/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Mid-size car or larger</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Planned shifts</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="taskrabbit">5. TaskRabbit and local tasks</h3>
    <p>Furniture assembly, mounting TVs, moving help and yard work. You set your own hourly rate and accept jobs from the app. It pays better than delivery once you have reviews, but you need basic tools and skills.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$60/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Tools, registration fee</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Handy people</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="pet-sitting">6. Pet sitting and dog walking</h3>
    <p>Rover and Wag match you with pet owners nearby. Overnight boarding and house sitting pay far more than single walks, and repeat clients become the backbone of the business.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$30/walk</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Animal lovers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="babysitting">7. Babysitting</h3>
    <p>Care.com and Sittercity let families find you. Date nights, school pickups and summer care are steady demand. Background checks and CPR certification help you get booked.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$25/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Background check</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Students, parents</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>

