<h2 id="spot-scams">How to spot phone side hustle scams</h2>
<p>Text-message "job offers" for remote phone work have exploded, and the FTC has repeatedly warned about task scams that pay small amounts at first, then ask you to deposit money to unlock bigger earnings. Use these rules:</p>

<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>You never pay to get paid.</strong> Legit gig apps may charge a background check or a one-time registration fee in some areas, but none require crypto deposits, "activation" payments or buying starter kits.</li>
  <li><strong>Real platforms do not recruit by WhatsApp or text.</strong> If a stranger messages you about "liking videos" or "rating products" for $300 a day, it is a scam.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch for overpayment checks.</strong> Mystery shopping and babysitting scams send a check for more than you are owed and ask you to send back the difference. The check bounces days later.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep payments inside the app.</strong> On Poshmark, eBay and Facebook Marketplace, buyers who push for Zelle, "verification codes" or shipping before payment are running a scam.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the app itself.</strong> Download from the official App Store or Google Play listing, look at the developer name, and read recent one-star reviews for payout complaints.</li>
  <li><strong>Be skeptical of pay that beats the market.</strong> Simple, no-skill tasks pay a few dollars an hour. Anything promising $50 an hour for tapping buttons is not real.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Never give a "recruiter" your bank login, SSN or a verification code sent to your phone before you have confirmed the company on its own official website.</div>

<h2 id="apps-to-get-started">Apps to get started</h2>
<p>If you only download five apps this week, make it a mix of fast cash and longer-term income:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fast cash:</strong> DoorDash or Uber Eats (instant cashout), Facebook Marketplace (sell what you own).</li>
  <li><strong>Stay-at-home income:</strong> UserTesting and Prolific.</li>
  <li><strong>Long-term skill:</strong> CapCut and Canva for UGC or social media clients, plus the Fiverr app to sell what you make.</li>
  <li><strong>Money management:</strong> A mileage tracker and a separate checking account so side hustle money does not blend into your daily spending.</li>
</ul>
<p>Want to level up a skill from your phone? <a href="/skillshare/">Skillshare</a> has short mobile-friendly classes on video editing, photography and social media.</p>

<h2 id="taxes">Don't forget taxes</h2>
<p>Phone hustle income is taxable even if you never receive a 1099. Payment apps and marketplaces now only send a 1099-K when you pass $20,000 and 200 transactions, so most side hustlers will not get one, but the income still counts. Set aside 25–30% of your profit, track deductible costs like mileage and the business share of your phone plan, and estimate what you owe with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</p>

<h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
<p>The best side hustles you can do from your phone are the ones that match your time and situation: gig apps if you have a car and evenings free, testing and reselling if you are home, and a sellable skill if you want income that grows. Not sure where you fit? Take the <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a> and Better Side Hustles will match you with options that suit your schedule.</p>
